Hi!

> But the point here is that you don't suspend the _subsystem_ (USB,
> block layer, network stack, whatever), but the individual drivers.

You are probably right at this.

> It do make sense to provide a way to stop requests. Requests are
> not always originated from userspace. Several kernel services can
> issue requests (VM, cache, whatever stuff on top of an i2c device,
> etc...) and you can't shut down those services because you don't
> have ordering rules for them, you only have ordering rules for
> the drivers.

All kernel services that can issue requests need kernel threads,
right? My approach is to take kernel threads one by one and make them
suspend-aware.
